From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 11:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] WASM: release the LOK headless frame at document close (NTH-10)

One LibreOffice frame leaked per conversion.

Every LOK documentLoad goes through loadComponentFromURL(url, "_blank", ...)
(desktop/source/lib/init.cxx), which registers a FRESH task frame in
Desktop::m_aChildTaskContainer.  Natively that frame is closed on the way down
through SfxBaseController::dispose() -> SfxFrame::DoClose_Impl().  Under WASM it
never is, because of the two patches that make headless conversion possible:

  * 20260209_054000_headless-controller-wasm.patch builds SfxBaseController with
    a null SfxViewShell, so SfxBaseController::dispose() hits its early return
    ("if (!m_pData->m_pViewShell) return;") BEFORE the model disconnect, the
    attachFrame(empty) and the DoClose_Impl() that would close the frame;
  * 20251221_203000_sfxbasemodel-lok-skip-viewframe.patch skips the SfxViewFrame
    wiring in SfxBaseModel::connectController() for the same reason.

Nothing was left that closes the frame.  `delete pDoc` in the bridge reaches
LibLODocument_Impl::~LibLODocument_Impl -> mxComponent->dispose() ->
SfxBaseModel::dispose() -> close(true), and SfxBaseModel::dispose() merely does
m_seqControllers.clear() -- it never disposes a controller.  So the XFrame, the
SfxBaseController it still references, and the closed model that controller
holds in m_xHeadlessModel all survived every document close, and one zombie
accumulated per conversion.  It went unnoticed only because the driver recycles
the worker after every conversion, capping the count at one per LOK instance.

Measured before the fix with tests/test_frame_leak.js, which drives ONE worker
directly (bypassing the driver's per-conversion recycle) and counts the
"[FRAME] LOK headless setComponent detach completed" lines emitted after
"[SHUTDOWN] shutdown_office() called" -- i.e. the frames Desktop::terminate() ->
impl_closeFrames() still finds alive:

    5 conversions  -> 5 frames created, 0 released, 5 alive at teardown
    10 conversions -> 10 frames created, 0 released, 10 alive at teardown

Fix: close the frame from SfxBaseModel::close(), the symmetric counterpart of
the connectController() skip.  It deliberately makes the SAME call that
impl_closeFrames() has always made on these frames at shutdown --
XCloseable::close(false), which routes into the BUG-20 headless detach fast path
(20260727_210000) -- so this moves an already-proven teardown to the right
moment rather than introducing a new one.  Frames are snapshotted before the
loop because closing one disposes its controller, which can mutate
m_seqControllers.  Re-entrancy is safe: m_bClosed is set true just above, so a
re-entrant close() returns immediately, and the following dispose() is guarded
by impl_isDisposed().  Any veto or DisposedException is swallowed and logged --
worst case that one frame reverts to the old behaviour and impl_closeFrames()
still collects it at shutdown, so a refusal can never block a document close.

Guarded by __EMSCRIPTEN__ + comphelper::LibreOfficeKit::isActive(); native
builds and non-LOK WASM paths are untouched.

Ordering note: this lands BEFORE NTH-09 (in-instance worker reuse).  Raising the
number of conversions per LOK instance is exactly what would turn this
per-conversion leak into a real problem.

diff --git a/sfx2/source/doc/sfxbasemodel.cxx b/sfx2/source/doc/sfxbasemodel.cxx
--- a/sfx2/source/doc/sfxbasemodel.cxx
+++ b/sfx2/source/doc/sfxbasemodel.cxx
@@ -23,6 +23,7 @@
 #include <chrono>
 #include <memory>
 #include <optional>
+#include <vector>
 #ifdef __EMSCRIPTEN__
 #include <iostream>
 #endif
@@ -43,6 +44,8 @@
 #include <com/sun/star/lang/NotInitializedException.hpp>
 #include <com/sun/star/frame/Desktop.hpp>
 #include <com/sun/star/frame/IllegalArgumentIOException.hpp>
+#include <com/sun/star/frame/XController.hpp>
+#include <com/sun/star/frame/XFrame.hpp>
 #include <com/sun/star/frame/XUntitledNumbers.hpp>
 #include <com/sun/star/frame/DoubleInitializationException.hpp>
 #include <com/sun/star/embed/XStorage.hpp>
@@ -1545,6 +1548,75 @@
     m_pData->m_bClosed = true;
     m_pData->m_bClosing = false;
 
+#ifdef __EMSCRIPTEN__
+    // WASM/LOK headless: release the frame this document was loaded into (NTH-10).
+    //
+    // Every LOK documentLoad goes through loadComponentFromURL(url, "_blank"),
+    // which registers a FRESH task frame in Desktop::m_aChildTaskContainer.
+    // Natively that frame is closed on the way down through
+    // SfxBaseController::dispose() -> SfxFrame::DoClose_Impl().  The WASM headless
+    // controller has no SfxViewShell, so dispose() takes its early return
+    // ("if (!m_pData->m_pViewShell) return;") long before that code, and
+    // connectController() above skips the SfxViewFrame wiring entirely for the
+    // same reason.  Nothing was left that closes the frame: SfxBaseModel::dispose()
+    // merely clears m_seqControllers without disposing anything, so the frame (and
+    // the controller it still references) survived every document close and one
+    // zombie accumulated per conversion.  Measured before this change with
+    // tests/test_frame_leak.js: 5 conversions in one LOK instance -> 5 frames still
+    // alive at teardown; 10 -> 10.
+    //
+    // Closing it here is the symmetric counterpart of the connectController() skip.
+    // It deliberately reuses the very call Desktop::terminate() -> impl_closeFrames()
+    // has always made on these frames at shutdown -- XCloseable::close(false), which
+    // routes into the BUG-20 headless detach fast path -- so this moves an
+    // already-proven teardown to the right moment instead of introducing a new one.
+    //
+    // Snapshot the frames first: closing one disposes its controller, which may
+    // mutate m_seqControllers.  Re-entrancy is safe -- m_bClosed is already true
+    // above, so a re-entrant close() returns immediately, and the dispose() below
+    // is guarded by impl_isDisposed().
+    if (comphelper::LibreOfficeKit::isActive())
+    {
+        std::vector< css::uno::Reference< css::frame::XFrame > > aFramesToClose;
+        for (const auto& rController : m_pData->m_seqControllers)
+        {
+            if (!rController.is())
+                continue;
+            try
+            {
+                css::uno::Reference< css::frame::XFrame > xFrame(rController->getFrame());
+                if (xFrame.is())
+                    aFramesToClose.push_back(xFrame);
+            }
+            catch (const css::uno::Exception&)
+            {
+            }
+        }
+        for (const auto& rFrame : aFramesToClose)
+        {
+            try
+            {
+                css::uno::Reference< css::util::XCloseable > xCloseFrame(rFrame, css::uno::UNO_QUERY);
+                if (xCloseFrame.is())
+                    xCloseFrame->close(false);
+                else
+                    rFrame->dispose();
+                std::cerr << "[SFXMODEL] LOK headless: frame released at document close"
+                          << std::endl << std::flush;
+            }
+            catch (const css::uno::Exception&)
+            {
+                // A frame that vetoes the close, or that is already gone, must never
+                // stop the document from closing.  Worst case we are back to the old
+                // behaviour for that one frame: impl_closeFrames() still catches it
+                // at shutdown.
+                std::cerr << "[SFXMODEL] LOK headless: frame close refused, leaving to teardown"
+                          << std::endl << std::flush;
+            }
+        }
+    }
+#endif
+
     dispose();
 }
 
